# LeakSanitizer suppressions — pam_oidc_device `make test-sanitize` gate ONLY.
#
# Every entry here documents *intentional, process-lifetime* third-party
# memory that a standalone test binary would otherwise report as a leak at
# exit. Each entry MUST be paired with a comment stating why it is not an
# application defect. Add a new entry only when a false positive is
# encountered; never use this file to suppress real application leaks.
#
# --- libcurl global state ---
# src/http.c's oauth_http_global_init() -> init_curl_global_impl() calls
# curl_global_init(CURL_GLOBAL_ALL) exactly once per process (via
# pthread_once) and, by design, never calls curl_global_cleanup(). This is
# the correct pattern for a long-lived PAM host (sshd): the PAM module is a
# shared library loaded into a persistent login process, and tearing down
# libcurl's global state in the middle of the process's life would break
# other components and any subsequent PAM calls. The standalone test binary
# links the same src/http.c translation unit and therefore always reports
# this process-global allocation as a leak when the test process exits.
# We anchor the suppression on our own frames (init_curl_global_impl /
# oauth_http_global_init) plus the two libcurl entry points that allocate
# the global state, so the suppression is stable across libcurl versions.
leak:init_curl_global_impl
leak:oauth_http_global_init
leak:curl_global_init
leak:Curl_init
